    "summary": "Mellow first set blooms into a soaring Wharf Rat > Half‑Step > Playing jam",
    "blurb": "The Boston Music Hall show opens with a relaxed, piano‑rich first set highlighted by the Weather Report Suite and solid folk‑rock numbers. The second set ignites with an unforgettable Wharf Rat → Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo → Playing In The Band sequence that launches into a soaring jam. Overall performance is high‑energy with standout moments throughout.",
    "review": "December 2, 1973 at Boston Music Hall finds the Dead in fine form, slipping from a gentle, Keith‑driven first set into one of their most rewarding second‑set journeys of the era. The Weather Report Suite glides with lush piano textures and Phil’s hypnotic bass, while Jerry’s lead work shines on the Prelude and Part 1. When the band launches into Wharf Rat, they immediately lock into a seamless segue through Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo into Playing In The Band, culminating in an expansive Mind Left Body jam that many fans cite as a high point of late‑’73 improvisation. The set closes with classic Dead staples—He’s Gone, Truckin’, Stella Blue and Sugar Magnolia—before a poignant Morning Dew encore. Across the available sources, the Dusborne matrix (gd1973-12-02.124133.mtx.dusborne.flac16) captures the full arc with superb ambience and crystal‑clear instrument separation, making it the go‑to version for both newcomers and completists.",
